00:21:32,120 --> 00:21:33,680
Explain that connection.

523
00:21:33,680 --> 00:21:36,120
How does visibility reduce anxiety?

524
00:21:36,120 --> 00:21:40,560
Well, imagine you hire a contractor to fix your kitchen,

525
00:21:40,560 --> 00:21:44,040
you pay them a deposit, and then silence.

526
00:21:44,040 --> 00:21:45,880
For three weeks, you don't see them,

527
00:21:45,880 --> 00:21:47,880
you don't hear from them, how do you feel?

528
00:21:47,880 --> 00:21:50,300
I feel like I've been scammed, I'm anxious,

529
00:21:50,300 --> 00:21:52,220
I'm checking my bank account, I'm calling them

530
00:21:52,220 --> 00:21:53,220
and they're not picking up.

531
00:21:53,220 --> 00:21:55,540
Exactly, even if they are working hard in their workshop,

532
00:21:55,540 --> 00:21:57,340
you don't see it, you have no visibility.

533
00:21:57,340 --> 00:22:00,500
In a corporation, managers are the homeowners.

534
00:22:00,500 --> 00:22:02,840
They are terrified that work isn't happening.

535
00:22:02,840 --> 00:22:05,740
Visibility is the act of constantly showing your work

536
00:22:05,740 --> 00:22:06,740
so they don't panic.

537
00:22:06,740 --> 00:22:07,740
So it's reassurance.

538
00:22:07,740 --> 00:22:09,060
Constant reassurance.

539
00:22:09,060 --> 00:22:12,000
Let's look at the onboarding workflow source.

540
00:22:12,000 --> 00:22:14,580
They're drafting a document for new hires.

541
00:22:14,580 --> 00:22:17,500
They use words like polishing, circulate,

542
00:22:17,500 --> 00:22:19,700
accountability and adoption.

543
00:22:19,700 --> 00:22:26,940
Polishing that is a great business English verb one speaker says we finish the first pass and are polishing the tone for clarity

544
00:22:27,060 --> 00:22:32,560
Think about the signal that sends if you tell your boss. I am writing it. What do they hear?

545
00:22:33,020 --> 00:22:35,540
That it's not done. They hear I haven't finished

546
00:22:35,900 --> 00:22:40,820
They might think you have barely started. It sounds heavy. It sounds like a lot of work is left

547
00:22:40,860 --> 00:22:46,340
But if you say I am polishing it, what are they here? They hear it's done. It just needs a shine

548
00:22:46,340 --> 00:22:54,080
It's almost ready to ship 99% done. It manages their expectations perfectly. It says relax the heavy lifting is over

549
00:22:54,080 --> 00:22:57,980
It creates a sense of completion then it says subtle but powerful distinction

550
00:22:58,340 --> 00:23:01,900
Framing the stage of work and then there's this discussion about the checklist

551
00:23:02,060 --> 00:23:06,720
Please add a checklist section for managers. It keeps accountability clear without adding extra burden

552
00:23:06,740 --> 00:23:09,820
That is the tightrope walk of management right there

553
00:23:09,820 --> 00:23:14,700
You want accountability you want to know people are doing their jobs, but you don't want to be a micromanager

554
00:23:14,700 --> 00:23:16,700
You don't want to be a burden.

555
00:23:16,700 --> 00:23:18,700
So how does a checklist solve that?

556
00:23:18,700 --> 00:23:20,700
It seems like it is a burden.

557
00:23:20,700 --> 00:23:22,700
It depersonalizes the enforcement.

558
00:23:22,700 --> 00:23:24,700
If I come to your desk every day and say,

559
00:23:24,700 --> 00:23:26,700
Did you do this? Did you do that?

560
00:23:26,700 --> 00:23:28,700
I'm annoying. I'm a burden.

561
00:23:28,700 --> 00:23:30,700
I am the bad guy.

562
00:23:30,700 --> 00:23:32,700
Right. I'd hate that.

563
00:23:32,700 --> 00:23:34,700
But if I give you a checklist,

564
00:23:34,700 --> 00:23:36,700
ideally the checklist is the bad guy,

565
00:23:36,700 --> 00:23:38,700
I'm just the helpful colleague giving you a tool.

566
00:23:38,700 --> 00:23:40,700
It shifts the authority

567
00:23:40,700 --> 00:23:42,700
from the person to the document.

568
00:23:42,700 --> 00:23:43,200
Oh!

569
00:23:43,200 --> 00:23:43,900
I'm not yelling at you.

570
00:23:43,900 --> 00:23:45,660
The checklist just says this needs to be done.

571
00:23:45,660 --> 00:23:46,500
Exactly.

572
00:23:46,500 --> 00:23:49,540
And look at the word adoption in that same text.

573
00:23:49,540 --> 00:23:52,940
That tone fits our culture and encourages adoption.

574
00:23:52,940 --> 00:23:55,580
They aren't just writing a document for the sake of writing it.

575
00:23:55,580 --> 00:23:57,100
They want people to actually use it.

576
00:23:57,100 --> 00:23:58,420
They want adoption.

577
00:23:58,420 --> 00:24:01,140
They want the user to actually use the product.

578
00:24:01,140 --> 00:24:03,060
This shows a strategic mindset.

579
00:24:03,060 --> 00:24:04,500
It is not enough to deliver.

580
00:24:04,500 --> 00:24:06,740
The delivery has to work for the end user.

581
00:24:06,740 --> 00:24:09,820
Which leads us to the launch window crisis source.

582
00:24:09,820 --> 00:24:12,140
This is where things are not going smooth.

583
00:24:12,140 --> 00:24:13,900
They were at risk of missing a deadline,

584
00:24:13,900 --> 00:24:16,380
and you can feel the tension in the text.

585
00:24:16,380 --> 00:24:18,940
The launch window is kite, one environment keeps failing.

586
00:24:18,940 --> 00:24:22,540
This is a classic red status situation, high stress.

587
00:24:22,540 --> 00:24:25,700
But look at the language they use to handle that stress.

588
00:24:25,700 --> 00:24:26,820
They don't panic.

589
00:24:26,820 --> 00:24:30,700
They use very specific tools, surface blockers.

590
00:24:30,700 --> 00:24:33,140
Visibility, trade-off.

591
00:24:33,140 --> 00:24:34,660
Let's start with surface blockers.

592
00:24:34,660 --> 00:24:36,580
The source says quick checkpoints

593
00:24:36,580 --> 00:24:38,540
help us surface blockers early.

594
00:24:38,540 --> 00:24:40,060
It's another visual metaphor.

595
00:24:40,060 --> 00:24:42,420
A blocker isn't just a problem.

596
00:24:42,420 --> 00:24:43,420
A problem is vague.

597
00:24:43,420 --> 00:24:44,820
I have a problem with the code.

598
00:24:44,820 --> 00:24:45,660
Okay, what does that mean?

599
00:24:45,660 --> 00:24:46,780
Are you stuck?

600
00:24:46,780 --> 00:24:47,620
Is it slow?

601
00:24:47,620 --> 00:24:48,660
It's not specific enough.

602
00:24:48,660 --> 00:24:49,500
No.

603
00:24:49,500 --> 00:24:51,660
A blocker implies a binary state.

604
00:24:51,660 --> 00:24:52,620
Movement is stopped.

605
00:24:52,620 --> 00:24:54,860
I cannot proceed until this is removed.

606
00:24:54,860 --> 00:24:55,780
It demands action.

607
00:24:55,780 --> 00:24:56,660
It screams for help.

608
00:24:56,660 --> 00:24:57,500
Yes.

609
00:24:57,500 --> 00:25:00,860
And surfacing, it means bringing it up from the deep.

610
00:25:00,860 --> 00:25:02,220
Think of a submarine.

611
00:25:02,220 --> 00:25:05,180
You need to surface the issue where everyone can see it.

612
00:25:05,180 --> 00:25:06,900
If you hide a problem in business,

613
00:25:06,900 --> 00:25:08,180
if you think, oh, I'll fix it

614
00:25:08,180 --> 00:25:11,180
before anyone notices that is dangerous.

615
00:25:11,180 --> 00:25:12,740
Because if you can't fix it.

616
00:25:12,740 --> 00:25:14,860
If you fail, you fail alone.

617
00:25:14,860 --> 00:25:18,540
If you surface the blocker, the team owns the problem.

618
00:25:18,540 --> 00:25:20,020
That connects back to visibility.

619
00:25:20,020 --> 00:25:23,140
The source says visibility makes coordination smoother.

620
00:25:23,140 --> 00:25:26,260
In a crisis, silence is terrifying.

621
00:25:26,260 --> 00:25:27,460
If I don't know what you're doing,

622
00:25:27,460 --> 00:25:30,340
I assume the worst, I assume you're failing.

623
00:25:30,340 --> 00:25:33,380
Visibility means keeping everything in the open.

624
00:25:33,380 --> 00:25:36,980
The source mentions a shared board or daily checkpoint.

625
00:25:36,980 --> 00:25:38,920
I will set a daily checkpoint.

626
00:25:38,920 --> 00:25:39,920
Daily.

627
00:25:39,920 --> 00:25:41,760
That is high frequency.

628
00:25:41,760 --> 00:25:44,120
In peacetime, you talk once a week.

629
00:25:44,120 --> 00:25:46,400
In wartime crisis time, you talk every day.

630
00:25:46,400 --> 00:25:47,520
Daily checkpoint.

631
00:25:47,520 --> 00:25:48,520
It sounds intense.

632
00:25:48,520 --> 00:25:51,080
It is intense, but it prevents drift.

633
00:25:51,080 --> 00:25:53,800
If you wait a week, you might drift 10 miles off course.

634
00:25:53,800 --> 00:25:56,000
If you check in daily, you can correct the course

635
00:25:56,000 --> 00:25:57,360
when you're only one inch off.

636
00:25:57,360 --> 00:25:58,760
It keeps the team tight.

637
00:25:58,760 --> 00:26:00,880
And then, when they realize they might actually

638
00:26:00,880 --> 00:26:04,000
miss the deadline, they don't just say, we failed.

639
00:26:04,000 --> 00:26:05,120
They use the trade-off.

640
00:26:05,120 --> 00:26:06,320
This is brilliant.

641
00:26:06,320 --> 00:26:10,220
If testing slips, we may need to drop one minor feature.

642
00:26:10,220 --> 00:26:11,920
I will prepare a simple trade-off note.

643
00:26:11,920 --> 00:26:13,200
What is a trade-off note?

644
00:26:13,200 --> 00:26:14,440
It's a menu of pain.

645
00:26:14,440 --> 00:26:15,920
You're telling leadership, okay,

646
00:26:15,920 --> 00:26:18,080
we have a constraint, reality has hit us,

647
00:26:18,080 --> 00:26:20,320
we can either launch late with everything

648
00:26:20,320 --> 00:26:22,440
or launch on time with one less thing.

649
00:26:22,440 --> 00:26:23,280
You choose.

650
00:26:23,280 --> 00:26:25,400
It's just the agency back to the decision-maker.

651
00:26:25,400 --> 00:26:26,240
Exactly.

652
00:26:26,240 --> 00:26:27,880
You aren't presenting a failure.

653
00:26:27,880 --> 00:26:30,040
You're presenting a strategic choice.

654
00:26:30,040 --> 00:26:32,640
You look like a partner in the business,

655
00:26:32,640 --> 00:26:34,280
not a victim of circumstance.

656
00:26:34,280 --> 00:26:36,280
That is so much better than just saying sorry

657
00:26:36,280 --> 00:26:43,480
were late. It's proactive. It frames the delay as a conscious decision rather than an operational

658
00:26:43,480 --> 00:26:48,440
collapse. There is a phrase in the launch window crisis I want to highlight. I will keep the shared

659
00:26:48,440 --> 00:26:54,300
board updated with the latest status. And the expert response in the source is, visibility makes

660
00:26:54,300 --> 00:26:59,660
coordination smoother. The shared board, whether it's Trello, Jira, Asana, whatever tool they are

661
00:26:59,660 --> 00:27:05,420
using, it is the single source of truth. Why is that so important in a crisis? In a crisis,

662
00:27:05,420 --> 00:27:07,420
It's the scoreboard. If it's not on the board, it isn't happening.

663
00:27:07,420 --> 00:27:12,420
It is. And updating it daily, as the source suggests, that is discipline.

664
00:27:12,420 --> 00:27:17,420
It tells the team, I am in control, even if things are breaking, I am watching them break.

665
00:27:17,420 --> 00:27:18,420
Okay, let's keep moving.

666
00:27:18,420 --> 00:27:22,420
Section 3. The vocabulary of risk and mitigation.

667
00:27:22,420 --> 00:27:26,420
This feels related to the crisis stuff, but this is more about looking ahead, right?

668
00:27:26,420 --> 00:27:27,420
Yes.

669
00:27:27,420 --> 00:27:30,450
Crisis management is a very important part of the process.

670
00:27:30,450 --> 00:27:32,050
This feels related to the crisis stuff,

671
00:27:32,050 --> 00:27:33,670
but this is more about looking ahead, right?

672
00:27:33,670 --> 00:27:34,490
Yes.

673
00:27:34,490 --> 00:27:36,970
Crisis management is when the house is on fire.

674
00:27:36,970 --> 00:27:39,890
Risk and mitigation is shucking the smoke detectors.

675
00:27:39,890 --> 00:27:40,890
We have a source here

676
00:27:40,890 --> 00:27:43,170
from a steering committee risk review.

677
00:27:43,170 --> 00:27:44,170
Very formal.

678
00:27:44,170 --> 00:27:46,650
The key words here are mitigate,

679
00:27:46,650 --> 00:27:50,600
bandwidth, risk register, and cross train.

680
00:27:50,600 --> 00:27:52,100
Let's talk about bandwidth.

681
00:27:52,100 --> 00:27:54,260
The source says, I will check their bandwidth.

682
00:27:54,260 --> 00:27:55,860
I know some people hate this word.

683
00:27:55,860 --> 00:27:57,260
They say it makes humans sound

684
00:27:57,260 --> 00:27:59,660
like internet cables or machines.

685
00:27:59,660 --> 00:28:02,300
It is a bit dehumanizing, my bandwidth is low.

686
00:28:02,300 --> 00:28:03,780
I always feel a bit silly saying it.

687
00:28:03,780 --> 00:28:05,780
It is, but in business English,

688
00:28:05,780 --> 00:28:08,180
it serves a very specific function.

689
00:28:08,180 --> 00:28:11,940
It turns human energy and time into a measurable quantity.

690
00:28:11,940 --> 00:28:15,580
You aren't asking, is he stressed or is he tired

691
00:28:15,580 --> 00:28:17,660
or is he going through a divorce?

692
00:28:17,660 --> 00:28:19,660
Which you probably shouldn't be asking anyway.

693
00:28:19,660 --> 00:28:22,380
Right, you're asking, does he have the capacity

694
00:28:22,380 --> 00:28:24,060
to take on more data?

695
00:28:24,060 --> 00:28:26,780
It removes the emotion from the workload discussion.

696
00:28:26,780 --> 00:28:28,020
It's just math.

697
00:28:28,020 --> 00:28:30,960
Exactly, if I say I don't have the bandwidth,

698
00:28:30,960 --> 00:28:33,620
it's a professional way of saying I cannot do this.

699
00:28:33,620 --> 00:28:34,700
It protects me.

700
00:28:34,700 --> 00:28:37,720
It's not a personal refusal, it's a resource limitation.

701
00:28:37,720 --> 00:28:41,700
It's easier to say than I'm overwhelmed or I'm too busy.

702
00:28:41,700 --> 00:28:42,620
It sounds objective.

703
00:28:42,620 --> 00:28:44,480
And then we have mitigate.

704
00:28:44,480 --> 00:28:47,300
Can we mitigate that by offering clearer specs?

705
00:28:47,300 --> 00:28:49,580
Mitigate is essential.

706
00:28:49,580 --> 00:28:52,180
You rarely solve risks in business completely.

707
00:28:52,180 --> 00:28:54,540
You can't make them zero, the market might crash,

708
00:28:54,540 --> 00:28:56,940
the server might melt, people might quit.

709
00:28:56,940 --> 00:28:58,420
So you can only lessen the impact.

710
00:28:58,420 --> 00:28:59,820
You can only mitigate them,

711
00:28:59,820 --> 00:29:01,940
reduce their severity or likelihood.

712
00:29:01,940 --> 00:29:03,740
It's a word that shows you're realistic,

713
00:29:03,740 --> 00:29:05,580
you know things might go wrong,

714
00:29:05,580 --> 00:29:07,620
but you have a plan to soften the blow.

715
00:29:07,620 --> 00:29:09,860
And one of those plans mentioned in the source

716
00:29:09,860 --> 00:29:12,060
is to cross-train.

717
00:29:12,060 --> 00:29:14,420
Can we cross-crain someone from operations?

718
00:29:14,420 --> 00:29:16,760
This is a classic mitigation strategy.

719
00:29:16,760 --> 00:29:19,020
If only one person knows how to do the job,

720
00:29:19,020 --> 00:29:20,940
that is a single point of failure.

721
00:29:20,940 --> 00:29:22,180
Another great business phrase,

722
00:29:22,180 --> 00:29:24,060
though not in this specific text.

723
00:29:24,060 --> 00:29:26,700
What if that person gets sick, what if they quit?

724
00:29:26,700 --> 00:29:27,700
The bus factor.

725
00:29:27,700 --> 00:29:28,820
The bus factor.

726
00:29:28,820 --> 00:29:32,220
How many people have to get hit by a bus for the project to fail?

727
00:29:32,220 --> 00:29:34,300
Morbid, but accurate.

728
00:29:34,300 --> 00:29:36,380
Cross training increases the bus factor.

729
00:29:36,380 --> 00:29:37,380
It creates redundancy.

730
00:29:37,380 --> 00:29:40,940
I want to expand on the cross training discussion.

731
00:29:40,940 --> 00:29:43,760
The source connects it to bandwidth.

732
00:29:43,760 --> 00:29:47,040
I will check their bandwidth and discuss with their lead.

733
00:29:47,040 --> 00:29:49,640
This is the politics of resource sharing.

734
00:29:49,640 --> 00:29:52,160
You can't just steal an employee from another team.

735
00:29:52,160 --> 00:29:53,960
You have to check their bandwidth.

736
00:29:53,960 --> 00:29:54,960
Do they have time?

737
00:29:54,960 --> 00:29:55,960
Yeah.

738
00:29:55,960 --> 00:30:01,560
with their lead ask permission. It respects the hierarchy. You don't want to step on toes. Yes.

739
00:30:01,560 --> 00:30:06,360
And Crosstrain implies a benefit for the employee too. It's not just do extra work,

740
00:30:06,360 --> 00:30:11,560
it's learn a new skill. We are going to Crosstrain you. It sounds like a development opportunity.

741
00:30:11,560 --> 00:30:15,560
Even if it's really just plugging a hole in the sinking ship. Right. That is the magic of

742
00:30:15,560 --> 00:30:20,040
business English. Right. Framing a staffing crisis as a learning opportunity. It's not

743
00:30:20,040 --> 00:30:21,880
Not cynical, it's efficient.

744
00:30:21,880 --> 00:30:27,080
It aligns the company's need, labor, with the employee's need, growth.

745
00:30:27,080 --> 00:30:30,640
Now there is a connection here to the risk register mentioned in the text.

746
00:30:30,640 --> 00:30:32,400
I will update the risk register.

747
00:30:32,400 --> 00:30:33,560
The risk register.

748
00:30:33,560 --> 00:30:35,600
It sounds like a dusty old book, but it's a shield.

749
00:30:35,600 --> 00:30:37,180
It's a paper trail.

750
00:30:37,180 --> 00:30:42,140
If a project fails because of a known risk, say, a vendor delay, and you didn't put

751
00:30:42,140 --> 00:30:44,320
it in the register, it's your fault.

752
00:30:44,320 --> 00:30:45,480
But if you wrote it down.

753
00:30:45,480 --> 00:30:49,120
But if you wrote it down three months ago, you can point to it and say, we

754
00:30:49,120 --> 00:30:51,240
We knew this might happen, we planned for it.

755
00:30:51,240 --> 00:30:54,000
I told you so, is not professional.

756
00:30:54,000 --> 00:30:56,320
It was in the risk registers, very professional.

757
00:30:56,320 --> 00:30:57,320
Precisely.

758
00:30:57,320 --> 00:31:00,280
It turns a complaint into a record.

759
00:31:00,280 --> 00:31:03,040
Now, what about when the priorities shift entirely?

760
00:31:03,040 --> 00:31:06,600
We have a source here called Shifting Priorities, the Sprint.

761
00:31:06,600 --> 00:31:10,080
The market request escalates and they have to change the plan.

762
00:31:10,080 --> 00:31:12,760
This is the agile mindset in action.

763
00:31:12,760 --> 00:31:15,000
The keyword here is dependencies.

764
00:31:15,000 --> 00:31:18,480
The alert system depends on the new API.

765
00:31:18,480 --> 00:31:23,520
dependencies is how you explain delays without taking the blame but also without throwing

766
00:31:23,520 --> 00:31:28,160
others under the bus. It's just a fact of the system. It's neutral. It is. We can't build the roof

767
00:31:28,160 --> 00:31:32,960
until the walls are up. The roof depends on the walls. It creates a logical chain of cause and

768
00:31:32,960 --> 00:31:39,530
effect. And when the dependency fails, the source mentions a fallback option. Let us identify a

769
00:31:39,530 --> 00:31:44,730
fallback option just in case. Plan B, but fallback sounds more technical, doesn't it? It sounds

770
00:31:44,730 --> 00:31:46,290
Sounds like a safety system.

771
00:31:46,290 --> 00:31:49,530
If the primary engine fails, the backup kicks in.

772
00:31:49,530 --> 00:31:50,930
It projects competence.

773
00:31:50,930 --> 00:31:53,210
It says, I have thought about failure,

774
00:31:53,210 --> 00:31:54,970
and I have prepared for it.

775
00:31:54,970 --> 00:31:56,530
And throughout all of this, the source

776
00:31:56,530 --> 00:31:59,010
emphasizes transparency.

777
00:31:59,010 --> 00:32:02,130
Transparency helps avoid confusion and builds trust.

778
00:32:02,130 --> 00:32:04,330
Transparency is the currency of trust

